# Build-context hygiene for the API image.
#
# `docker-compose.yml` gives the API build the repo root as its context
# (dockerfile apps/api/Dockerfile). The web app builds from its own context
# (apps/web) and is governed by apps/web/.dockerignore.
#
# This file exists because of what was measured without it: the host tree carries
# a 224,952,320-byte working SQLite database, a 264 MB CPython virtualenv, two
# old-pin virtualenvs, and 67 Windows-compiled *.pyc files. BuildKit only
# transfers what a COPY line reaches (10.23 MB for this image today), but it walks
# everything it is not told to ignore, and `COPY apps/api apps/api` does reach
# those *.pyc — so the shipped image ran host-built bytecode for a different OS.
# Python recompiles all of it on first import inside the container; nothing here
# is a source file.
#
# Never excluded: the three paths the Dockerfile COPYs — packages/contracts,
# apps/api, data/ — except for the build artefacts listed above inside them.

# --- host-only state, never in any image ---
.git
.github
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.venv
.venv-*
.hypothesis
.ruff_cache
**/*.log

# --- installed or generated artefacts; the build recreates these in-container ---
node_modules
**/node_modules
.next
**/.next
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/test-results
**/playwright-report

# --- runtime-written data stores ---
# The API writes MEDISAATHI_DB_PATH=/data/medisaathi.db on a named volume; the web
# app writes DATABASE_URL=file:/app/db/custom.db on another. A .db found in the
# tree is developer state from a previous run, not an asset — and demo plans are
# not something to bake into a published image.
**/*.db
**/*.db-journal
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3

# --- superseded corpus duplicates ---
# data/*.csv.bak are backups of the four engine CSVs. No engine or test path reads
# them (grep for `.bak` across apps/api and packages/contracts: no matches), and a
# stale copy in the image is only a way to verify the wrong data.
data/**/*.bak
